  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday afternoon and evening: An upper-level trough over the\nGulf of Alaska will eject a shortwave inland, pushing a dry\n\"cold\" front across the region late Tuesday afternoon. Tight \npressure gradients will maintain west to southwest winds through\nthe Cascade gaps, across the Waterville Plateau, into the \nColumbia Basin, and up the Okanogan Valley through Tuesday \nevening. Expect sustained winds around 15 to 20 mph and gusts of\n30 to 35 mph, decreasing around 8pm to 10pm. In the wake of the\nfront, precipitable water (PWAT) values will fall to 40 to 60 \npercent of normal. Fortunately the frontal passage will be late \nenough that cooling temperatures in the evening will help \nmitigate a drop in relative humidity during the period of \nelevated winds. However, minimum RHs will trend drier by \nWednesday afternoon, dropping into the low to mid teens for most\nlocations. Cooling behind the front will be minimal, with high\ntemperatures on Wednesday only dropping a degree or two compared\nto Tuesday, remaining in the mid to upper 80s.\n\nWednesday through Friday: Quiet conditions prevail Wednesday\nwith light winds. Upper-level ridging expanding north from the\nDesert Southwest will initiate a warming trend Thursday and\nFriday, driving afternoon high temperatures back into the upper\n80s to mid 90s. An incoming shortwave will will create localized\nbreezy conditions across Central Washington Thursday afternoon\nwith sustained speeds of 10 to 15 mph and gusts to 25 mph. The\nshortwave will also support a low (5 to 10 percent) chance of\nthunderstorms over the Blue Mountains and Central Idaho\nPanhandle, though the best moisture and dynamics for\nthunderstorm development will be further southeast. \n\nSaturday through Monday: Saturday continues to be the primary\nfocus of the next seven days. An upper-level low offshore of\nBritish Columbia will swing inland, driving a strong dry cold\nfront through the Pacific Northwest and positioning an\nimpressive upper-level jet (120+ kts at 200 mb) directly over\nthe area. Model guidance continues to trend upward with surface\nwind forecasts. Probabilities for wind gusts exceeding 40 mph\nhave risen to 70 to 100 percent across the Columbia Basin,\nWaterville Plateau, Blue Mountains, and Lewiston-Clarkston\nValley. Over the Waterville Plateau where the strongest winds\nwill be focused, the National Blend of Models indicates a 50 to\n70 percent chance of gusts exceeding 50 mph. These impressive\nwinds combined with minimum RH values in the upper teens to low\n20s will bring widespread critical fire weather conditions.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, a sharp cooldown will limit highs\non Sunday to the mid to upper 70s, around 10 degrees cooler than\non Saturday. West winds will decrease some compared to Saturday\nbut will remain breezy, with sustained speeds of 10 to 20 mph\nand gusts of 25 to 35 mph. A shortwave ridge of high pressure\nmoves in on Monday, bringing temperatures back into the low to\nmid 80s with light winds forecast across the region. /Fewkes",
